ry so that when we  have a suspect in custody we ensure that  they are breathing and their breathing  is not restricted when they're in  custody  mandating a duty to intervene and a duty  to report  when an officer sees another officer or  supervisor sees an officer  doing something improper providing more  direction  in what de-escalation really means and  training our folks  on how to de-escalate making that part  of the fabric of everything we do  every call we go on we talk about the  escalation  we've prohibited choke holds and neck  restraints  we've made changes and we've learned  over time that we have a responsibility  to listen to our community  to you to learn from our community and  to change  the strength of our police department  comes from this community  it comes from you the public among our  many duties to the public  we're here to protect the first  amendment right  to assemble protest some of you may  choose to exercise that right today  and we stand here with you the community  to ensure  hand in hand that they remain peaceful  and the message is heard  this last year has really illustrated a  number  of issues that really impact our  community  and the death of mr floyd was a tragic  event  that should not have happened  having said that today a jury has spoken  and held a police officer accountable  for their conduct  our hope at the lvmpd as we continue to  work collaboratively  and we continue to earn the public's  trust  so i want to thank you all for being  here i want to thank the members of the  community  i want to thank my officers that are  here as well as  members of the media and the public i am  uh willing to take some questions if you  have any questions with me today  a couple questions talk you notice from  channel 13. i'm  curious going forward will you guys be  using this case the derek show the case  in your training now moving forward well  i will tell you this that's not within  our training  and i think there's not a person on this  planet  that looks at that and thinks that  that's okay so that's never been part of  our training or will it be part of our  training but yes  obviously the illustration of what  not following policy results  in i think sends a powerful message  yes sir police officer nasa civilian  community member what was your feeling  when you saw that video and what was you  feeling today when the jury came back  guilty well i will tell you  the day that i first saw that video i  had another employee who's now a deputy  chief come into my office and said you  have to see this video it's the most  disturbing thing i've ever seen  and he says i think a city is going to  burn and  i looked at it i thought i was just  shocked so it was just utter shock and  disappointment  and then today i would say that the uh  feeling is relief  yes many peoples they are calling this  the first real victory when it comes to  seeing an officer  uh held accountable in this country for  the death of an african-american  um also during this trial we've heard a  lot of talk about the blue wall of  silence  what is metro police's reaction to the  conversation that's happened about the  blue wall of silence  and what is being done to make sure that  officers  one thing to you know mandate is to make  sure the officers are actually reporting  when something happens correct so a  couple things to that  number one we don't tolerate it um we  mandate that it does not occur  and we put it in policy but like i said  you have to change a culture so the only  way to change that culture is to enforce  the policy and hold those accountable  that don't follow it  right we train our folks we tell them  what the standard is  we provide them direction on what to do  and how to really attain that standard  and now we have a policy that really  nails it down  and then we follow through and hold them  accountable and we have tracking  mechanisms obviously when we see people  that have complaints we'll hold t